/**

\page tetrahedral_flips Local Flips on Tetrahedral Meshes

For local mesh optimization, %OpenVolumeMesh provides two in-place
topological edits on tetrahedral meshes: the 2-to-3 and the 3-to-2 flip.
Neither of them adds vertices or changes vertex coordinates, and both are
implemented directly on the mesh instead of producing a separate result
object.

\subsection flip23 2-to-3 Flip

A 2-to-3 flip replaces the two tetrahedra that share an internal
triangular face by three tetrahedra arranged around a new internal edge.
The new edge connects the two vertices that were opposite to the shared
face. The six faces forming the cavity hull keep their handles, their
half-face orientations and all of their properties; only the old internal
face and the two old cells are marked deleted.

Call TetrahedralMeshTopologyKernel::flip23() on a
TetrahedralMeshTopologyKernel, or the geometric overload on a
TetrahedralGeometryKernel:

\code
TetrahedralGeometryKernel<Vec3d> mesh;
// ... build two tets sharing an internal face fh ...

std::vector<CellHandle> created;
if (mesh.flip23(fh, created)) {
    // created contains the three new cell handles, sorted ascending
}
\endcode

\subsection flip32 3-to-2 Flip

The 3-to-2 flip is the exact inverse: it replaces the three tetrahedra
around an internal edge of valence three by two tetrahedra that share a
new internal triangular face. The new face connects the three vertices
opposite to the central edge (the vertices of the edge's link).

\code
std::vector<CellHandle> created;
if (mesh.flip32(edge, created)) {
    // created contains the two new cell handles, sorted ascending
}
\endcode

Because the two operations are inverse, a successful 2-to-3 flip can be
undone immediately by a 3-to-2 flip on the freshly created edge, and any
number of flips can be chained. Cell-vertex traversal, per-half-face
adjacency and boundary queries reflect the new connectivity right after
each call.

\subsection flip_requirements Requirements and Rejection

Both flips require deferred deletion and all bottom-up incidences
(vertex, edge and face) to be enabled; otherwise they return false
without touching the mesh. They never toggle these switches and never run
garbage collection.

The topological kernel only checks combinatorial validity. A flip is
rejected, with the mesh left unchanged, when:

\li the target face is on the boundary (2-to-3), or the target edge is on
    the boundary or does not have valence three (3-to-2);
\li the five cavity vertices are not pairwise distinct;
\li the cavity does not have a spherical link, e.g. because of
    self-adjacent cells;
\li duplicate edges or faces already occupy a candidate internal
    simplex;
\li a handle is invalid or already deleted.

On failure the output vector, the entity counts, all properties and all
deletion flags stay as they were before the call.

\subsection flip_geometry Geometric Volume Gate

TetrahedralGeometryKernel offers geometric overloads with an additional
relative volume threshold (default \f$10^{-12}\f$):

\code
const double threshold = 1e-12;
mesh.flip23(fh, created, threshold);
mesh.flip32(edge, created, threshold);
\endcode

Before writing, every removed cell must have a strictly positive signed
volume, and every candidate replacement tet must satisfy
\f$V > \epsilon\,L^3\f$, where \f$L\f$ is the longest distance between the
five cavity vertices. Comparing the signed (not the absolute) volume
ensures that a tet folding out of the cavity cannot pass the gate. Since
the threshold is relative to \f$L^3\f$, the test is independent of the
mesh's length scale.

\subsection flip_properties Handles and Properties

The six hull faces keep their handles and half-face orientations, so any
external references to them remain valid. New internal edges and faces
receive default property values. Every replacement cell copies all cell
properties of the removed cell with the smallest handle, including
user-defined property types. Removed cells and internal simplices are
only marked deleted; their slots remain available until an explicit
garbage collection, which the flips never trigger.

*/
